    fullReview:
      "I have owned an Italian restaurant on the north side of Chicago for fifteen years. Anyone who has run a restaurant knows the math: you can do everything right, pack the dining room, get good press, and still land on a 5 to 8 percent margin. One bad winter, one equipment failure, one change in food costs, and a good year becomes a break-even year. The pandemic made that lesson permanent for me. I came out of it with the business intact but with a conviction that I could not have every dollar of my net worth tied to one room on one street. Diversifying was the goal. The problem was that I already work six days a week and I am the person who solves every problem in the building. I did not have capacity for a second business, and I have watched enough friends buy rental properties and turn into part-time plumbers to know what I was avoiding. A friend of mine who owns a couple of dry cleaners showed me his BnB Accelerator statements over dinner at my own bar. He is not a real estate guy. He just wrote a check and their team did the rest. That is the part I needed to understand clearly before I moved: this is not a course, and it is not coaching. Nobody was going to teach me how to be an Airbnb host. Their firm acquires the properties, furnishes them, launches them, and operates them. As an owner, I do the same thing I do with my linen vendor and my accountant. I hire competent people and I check the numbers. I had about $65,000 to deploy. Their market team recommended Nashville, and their case was straightforward: diversified demand from tourism, music, conventions, and bachelor and bachelorette travel, with strong midweek performance rather than a market that only works on Saturdays. Their acquisition team secured a loft in East Nashville, furnished it for $5,600, and launched it. First month it produced $3,800 net. To put that in restaurant terms, that is a very good week of profit arriving without a single hour of labor from me. A few months later they added a one-bedroom near the Gulch that runs about $3,300 per month. Combined, the two properties clear $7,100 monthly, and my total involvement is opening the dashboard on my phone during the afternoon lull between lunch and dinner service. Nine months in, I have not talked to a guest, hired a cleaner, or approved a repair. Their team handles all of it and sends me a statement. For the first time in fifteen years, I have income that does not depend on how many covers we do on a Tuesday. If the restaurant has a slow quarter now, it is a slow quarter, not a crisis. I still love the restaurant. I just no longer need it to carry everything.",
